<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01NR">Remark <span data-tag="01NR">4.6.6.2</span>.</a> In the situation of Notation <a data-tag="04HZ" href="/tag/04HZ">4.6.6.1</a>, we can also identify $f_{\pm }$ with a morphism of simplicial sets $\widetilde{f}_{+}: K_{+} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ f_{-} / }$. Let $Y$ be any simplicial set. Using Proposition <a data-tag="0189" href="/tag/0189">4.3.5.13</a> we see that the following data are equivalent: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(1)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Morphisms from $Y$ to $( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ / f_{+} } )_{ \overline{f}_{-} / }$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(2)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Morphisms from $Y$ to $( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ f_{-} /} )_{ / \overline{f}_{+} }$. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(3)$</dt>
<dd><p>
Morphisms $f: K_{-} \star Y \star K_{+} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ satisfying $f|_{ K_{-} \star K_{+} } = f_{\pm }$. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> It follows that the simplicial set $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ f_{-} / \,  / f_{+} } = ( \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ / f_{+} } )_{ \widetilde{f}_{-} / }$ can also be identified with the slice simplicial set $(\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}_{ f_{-} / })_{ /\widetilde{f}_{+} }$. </p>
</article>
